<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App Transport Security is off, deliberately.

	  This app's entire purpose is sending requests the user composes, to
	  whatever host they type. ATS's default refusal of plain HTTP would make
	  http://localhost:8080 unreachable — which is where an API being developed
	  usually lives, and where its OpenAPI document usually lives with it. The
	  same applies to reading a document over http.

	  The trade-off is real and worth naming: nothing this app sends is checked
	  for transport security any more, so a request to an http:// URL travels in
	  the clear. That is the correct behaviour for an HTTP client — it is the
	  user's call which host and scheme to use, and every other API client works
	  this way — but it is a deliberate choice, not an oversight.

	  Everything else in this file is generated: GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on
	  and merges the INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings into this one.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
